# {{ project_name }}

{{ project_description }}

This project is a portable MMIXAL application running on Donald Knuth's MMIX
reference architecture. The application artifact is `build/app/{{ project_name }}.mmo`,
not a host-native executable. All validation tools and test fixtures are local.
After generation, `just build`, `just test`, `just coverage`, `just ci`, and
`just ci-quiet` work without network access.

## Prerequisites

- Git and `just` 1.32 or newer.
- macOS: Clang / `cc`; Linux: `cc`, Clang, or GCC.
- Windows: MSVC `cl.exe` with C11 support in a Visual Studio Developer PowerShell, with the Windows SDK.
- The system POSIX shell on macOS/Linux; built-in Windows PowerShell on Windows.

`CC` takes precedence and must name one compiler executable, not a shell command
or a string containing compiler flags. Unix detection tries `cc`, `clang`, then
`gcc`; Windows uses `cl.exe`. `just check` prints the selected compiler and platform
and checks host prerequisites only. It never installs anything.

Copier/Python is required only by the blueprint generator, not by this project.
No global MMIXware installation, CWEB, package manager, or additional language
runtime is needed for validation.

## Start

```text
just check
just init
just test
just run 4
just coverage
just ci-quiet
```

The sample accepts exactly one digit from `0` through `8`, prints the next digit
and a newline, and exits zero. Invalid input writes a diagnostic to stderr and
exits 1. Missing or extra arguments print usage to stderr and exit 2.
The same MMIX instructions and byte-exact golden files run on every platform.

`just init` initializes Git, installs the local `just ci-quiet` pre-commit hook,
builds host tools, and executes the success smoke test. `just destroy` removes only disposable `build/` output.

## Commands

| Group | Commands |
| --- | --- |
| Setup | `init`, `check`, `destroy`, `help` |
| Build/run | `build`, `assemble`, `run`, `debug` |
| Code | `code-style`, `code-source`, `code-vendor`, `code-object` |
| Boundaries | `code-architecture`, `code-security` |
| Tests | `test`, `test-unit`, `test-state`, `test-cli`, `selftest` |
| Coverage/CI | `coverage`, `ci`, `ci-quiet` |

`just debug` uses MMIX's own interactive simulator. No external debugger is used.
`assemble` validates and assembles, rejects diagnostics, emits an MMO object and
an MML listing, and inspects them; it does not execute behavioral tests.
`ci-quiet` runs exactly the same stages as `ci`, prints each stage's status, and
prints the full captured stdout/stderr of any failing stage.

## Tooling matrix

| Concern | Mechanism |
| --- | --- |
| Syntax | Vendored `mmixal` |
| Execution | Vendored `mmix` |
| Object inspection | Vendored `mmotype` |
| Source policy | Local `mmix-guard` |
| Capability control | Exact TRAP, special-register, and `$255` allowlists |
| Architecture | Local source-boundary rules |
| Unit testing | Independently assembled MMIX test programs |
| State testing | Scripted MMIX register, memory, PC, and termination inspection |
| CLI testing | Declarative `.test` fixtures with exact output bytes |
| Coverage | Real MMIX execution profiles and executable listing addresses |
| Vendor integrity | Local SHA-256 manifest and exact file inventory |
| CI | Same C driver on macOS, Linux, and Windows |

There is no maintained semantic MMIXAL static analyzer in this template. MMIXAL
provides authoritative assembly validation; repository-local guardrails enforce
project policy; simulator-based tests verify behavior.

Mutation testing: not provided. Secret detection and dependency CVE scanning are
not provided. The project deliberately omits Semgrep, Gitleaks, CodeQL, codespell,
ShellCheck, shfmt, Docker, GNU binutils, external MMIX packages, third-party coverage
systems, hosted analysis, remote APIs, and package restoration.

## Source architecture and capabilities

Composition order is explicit: the application or unit-test entry, then
`src/lib/parse.mms`, `src/lib/format.mms`, and `src/lib/io.mms`. Each assembled
composition defines `Main` exactly once. Entries own data/global-register layout;
library files supply routines. Pure parse/format modules perform no TRAP operations.
Only `io.mms` crosses the configured runtime capability boundary.

`config/guardrails.conf` defines source conventions and scratch-register policy.
`config/allowed-traps.txt`, `allowed-special-registers.txt`, and `allowed-255.txt`
contain exact project permissions. Used permissions must equal configured
permissions: unused or unknown capabilities fail. Source comments cannot suppress
checks. Numeric constants are allowed; suspicious literal addresses and unexplained
reserved-register uses are rejected within the documented policy's limits.

MMIXAL is invoked without `-x`. Address construction must remain visible, and
listings are checked against assembled instruction structure. There is no
aggressive automatic formatter; `code-style` checks whitespace, final newlines,
UTF-8, source artifacts, and case collisions. Deliberately malformed parser fixtures
are test data, not production source or configuration.

## Tests and coverage

MMIX unit tests under `tests/unit` exercise parsing and formatting and exit nonzero
on mismatches. CLI tests under `tests/cli` declare `program`, `stdin`, `stdout`,
`stderr`, `exit`, and consecutive optional `arg.1`, `arg.2`, etc. An empty stream
value means an empty stream, not an omitted assertion. Expected output files are
committed under `tests/fixtures/expected` and compared byte-for-byte.

State tests under `tests/state` declare `run=until-halt`, argument values, hexadecimal
register/memory/PC expectations, and `termination=halted`. The driver invokes the
real simulator and rejects malformed state output or unexpected termination.

`coverage` aggregates production execution profiles from the CLI corpus against
executable addresses from the assembly listing. Data and directives are excluded
structurally. `config/coverage.conf` sets `minimum=80`. Missing, malformed, duplicate,
or inconsistent profile records fail. There are no comment-based coverage exclusions.
Do not lower the threshold to hide inadequate tests.

## Trusted local tools

The C11 guard sources live in `tools/guard`. They compile with strict warnings:
`-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror` on GCC/Clang and `/W4 /WX` on MSVC. Parsers have
committed self-test cases; processes are invoked with argument arrays, bounded
execution times, and checked exit status. No validation code uses network APIs.

MMIXware's generated C and upstream notices remain unmodified under
`vendor/mmixware`. Its legacy C compilation is separate from strict local guard
compilation; its diagnostics are preserved in `build/tmp/*-compiler.err` and `.out`.
`VERSION`, `UPSTREAM`, `SHA256SUMS`, and `PROVENANCE.md` identify the
pinned source. Generated `abstime.h` is committed and hash-verified, making the
simulator's metadata independent of the build clock. MMO creation timestamps are
not used as behavioral assertions or claimed to be byte-reproducible.

The Windows simulator adapter changes host standard streams to binary mode so MMIX
program output has identical bytes across platforms. It does not change application
behavior or upstream source. Bootstrap shell/PowerShell files only select a compiler,
check prerequisites, and compile the C driver; the validation pipeline has one C
implementation.

CI infrastructure may provision Git, a compiler, and `just`; generated validation
never provisions dependencies. The GitHub Actions matrix explicitly uses that policy.
Deleting `build/` and running `just ci` recompiles the guard and all MMIXware tools
from repository-contained inputs before running the complete validation suite.
